# Roles and permissions

Authentication tells you who is making a request. Authorization decides
what they may do. `howdy_auth` keeps the two apart: `howdy/authorization`
stores roles, the permissions each role grants, and which users hold which
roles.

A session does not carry roles. Every check reads the current grants, so
taking a role away works on the very next request, without waiting for
anybody's session to end.

## Setting up

Add `authorization.schema()` to your
[migrations](/docs/auth/migrations), after `auth.schema()`, and build one
`Authorization` at startup from the same Repo:

```gleam
import howdy/authorization as access

let assert Ok(permissions) = access.new(db)
```

## Defining and assigning roles

A role is a name and a list of permissions. Both are plain strings of your
choosing:

```gleam
import howdy/auth/user

let assert Ok(_) =
  access.define_role(
    permissions,
    access.Global,
    "editor",
    ["articles.read", "articles.write"],
    by: user.System,
  )

let assert Ok(_) =
  access.assign(
    permissions,
    user_id,
    "editor",
    access.Global,
    by: user.Acting(admin),
  )
```

- `define_role` creates a role, or replaces the permissions of an existing
  one. People who hold the role keep it.
- `assign` gives a role to a user, and `revoke` takes it away. A role or a
  user that does not exist is a `NotFound`. Revoking a role somebody does
  not hold is not an error.

These are trusted operations. None is exposed over HTTP, and there is no
automatic administrator: decide in your own code who may call them, for
instance from a startup task, or from a handler that has already checked a
permission. `by:` records who acted, and is `user.System` for code acting
for nobody.

## Checking

Inside a handler that has [authenticated](/docs/auth/overview) the request,
add a second guard:

```gleam
use _ <- guard.require(
  ctx,
  access.require_permission(permissions, "articles.write", access.Global),
)
```

A user without the permission gets `403`. `access.require_role(permissions,
"editor", access.Global)` checks for the role by name.

Prefer permissions. A handler that asks "may this user write articles?"
keeps working when you later split `editor` into two roles; one that asks
"is this user an editor?" does not.

Outside HTTP, `access.allowed(permissions, principal, "articles.write",
access.Global)` and `access.has_role` answer the same questions as a
`service.Result(Bool)`. A database failure is an error, never a `True`.

## The rules

- Somebody with several roles has the permissions of all of them.
- Names are literal. There is no `admin` that may do everything, no
  wildcards, no role that inherits from another, and no deny rules.
- Permissions that nobody has defined are simply not granted.

## Scopes

Every role and assignment lives in a scope. `access.Global` is the scope
for the whole app. `access.Organization(id)` is a scope for one
organization, and the natural id to use is a [group](/docs/auth/groups) id:

```gleam
let scope = access.Organization(principal.user.group_id)

use _ <- guard.require(
  ctx,
  access.require_permission(permissions, "articles.write", scope),
)
```

A grant only counts in exactly its own scope. A global role does not pass
an organization check, so define and assign roles in each scope you check.

Take the scope from something you have verified, such as the signed-in
user's group or the organization of the record being changed, and not from
a header the client chose. A passed check does not filter your queries
either: you still select only that organization's rows.

## Caching

By default every check reads the database. To allow a short cache:

```gleam
let assert Ok(permissions) = access.with_cache(permissions, seconds: 5)
```

The lifetime is 1 to 60 seconds. Changes made through this module on the
same node clear the cache at once, as do suspending and resuming a user.
Changes made on another node, or straight in SQL, can go unseen until the
lifetime runs out, so keep the default if a revocation must take effect
everywhere immediately.
